        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "5683a021-418c-538b-8fd9-59fa06348ddc",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Henry Bacon, Daniel Chester French, Abraham Lincoln\n- Places: Washington, D.C.\n- Projects: Lincoln Memorial, Perry Memorial Arch\n- Topics: American architecture, American sculpture, national monuments\n\nFacts:\n- Henry Bacon was an American Beaux-Arts architect who lived from November 28, 1866 to February 16, 1924.\n- Daniel Chester French was an American sculptor who lived from April 20, 1850 to October 7, 1931.\n- Henry Bacon designed the Perry Memorial Arch, which was built in 1918.\n- Daniel Chester French designed the monumental statue of Abraham Lincoln in the Lincoln Memorial in 1920.\n- The Lincoln Memorial in Washington, D.C., was built from 1915 to 1922 to honor Abraham Lincoln, the 16th President of the United States.\n- The Lincoln Memorial was dedicated in 1922 as one of several monuments built to honor an American president.\n- Henry Bacon is best remembered for the Lincoln Memorial in Washington, D.C., which was his final project.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "61a1434f-f5f0-56fd-b8bf-75aec4c1a74e",
